<DocsLayout
  title="Security graph console"
  description="Use the OpenSourceOM console to explore the security graph, findings, named attack paths, and identity blast radius."
>
  <h1>Security graph console</h1>
  <p>
    The API embeds a static console at <code>/</code> and <code>/ui/</code>. After
    <code>docker compose up</code> and a scan, open
    <a href="http://localhost:8080">http://localhost:8080</a>. The page loads stats, findings, and
    a vis-network graph of the snapshot.
  </p>

  <h2>What you can do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Read node and edge counts in the stats strip. Those numbers come from <code>GET /v1/graph/stats</code>.</li>
    <li>Scan the findings list from <code>GET /v1/findings</code>. Each card shows severity, title, and the affected resource. An attack-path card also prints the <code>path</code> node ids. Choosing a card highlights the affected resource when it is in the current snapshot.</li>
    <li>The dropdown loads every named query. On first paint it selects <code>internet-to-datastore</code> when that query exists, then draws those paths. Choose <strong>Full graph snapshot</strong> to call <code>GET /v1/graph/snapshot</code> instead (500 nodes and 2000 edges).</li>
    <li>A named query still uses the snapshot’s edge list to label each hop. If the connecting edge is past that 2000-edge cap, the hop is drawn as type <code>PATH</code> and any <code>reason</code> property is missing. The nodes themselves come from the query, not from the snapshot cap. Audit events returned in <code>audits</code> are listed under that path.</li>
    <li>Select an identity node to load <code>GET /v1/identity/blast-radius</code>. The panel shows the summary and the reachable nodes. See <a href="/docs/blast-radius/">Blast radius</a>. Other node types do not open that panel.</li>
    <li>Refresh reloads stats, findings, and the current view after you ingest from the CLI. The page does not poll.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Choosing a finding calls <code>selectNodes</code> on the canvas. That highlight only lands
    when the affected resource is in the view you have open. A finding about a node that is not
    on the current path, and not in the snapshot when you are on the full graph, stays in the
    list and is not drawn.
  </p>

  <h2>Sign in</h2>
  <p>
    The console signs people in with an email and password. There is no API-key field.
    While no admin exists, the page is a setup form. Keep the API port private until you
    finish it, or run <code>om admin create --email you@example.com</code> on the machine
    first. Set <code>OM_ADMIN_EMAIL</code> when that first address should be fixed ahead of
    time. See <a href="/docs/configuration/">Configuration</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    An admin adds the next person from the <strong>Admins</strong> panel. The temporary
    password is shown once. If SMTP is configured, it is emailed too. That person chooses
    their own password at the next sign-in. Every signed-in account is an admin, and the last
    admin cannot be removed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Google and GitHub buttons appear only when that provider is configured. Register
    <code>{'{OM_PUBLIC_URL}'}/auth/google/callback</code> or
    <code>{'{OM_PUBLIC_URL}'}/auth/github/callback</code>. A verified email from the provider
    must already be an admin. The <strong>Security</strong> panel turns on optional
    authenticator-app two-factor authentication.
  </p>
  <p>
    The browser keeps an httpOnly session cookie for 7 days. Creating an admin also creates a
    personal API token. The console shows it once. Scripts send that token as
    <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> or <code>X-API-Key</code>. The token cannot add or
    remove admins. Rotate it from the Admins panel when you need a new one.
  </p>

  <h2>Run CSPM rules from the browser</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>Run CSPM rules</strong> button calls <code>POST /v1/rules/run</code> with the
    session cookie. Running <code>om rules run</code> on the CLI does the same work without a
    browser session, because the CLI uses Postgres directly.
  </p>

  <h2>Graph script</h2>
  <p>
    The graph canvas loads vis-network from <code>/ui/vis-network.min.js</code>, served with the
    rest of the console. The API does not fetch that script from a CDN.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
